WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.475
Approval answers whether
something should be done.

2
00:00:02.475 --> 00:00:05.675
It doesn't answer whether the
caller is who they claim to be,

3
00:00:05.675 --> 00:00:07.675
and that's the gap attackers exploit.

4
00:00:07.675 --> 00:00:10.497
So the book builds a verification ladder,

5
00:00:10.497 --> 00:00:13.650
and it's the CISO's decision,
not the help desk's.

6
00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:17.099
If someone can still sign in with
a phishing-resistant method,

7
00:00:17.099 --> 00:00:18.925
that session proves who they are.

8
00:00:18.925 --> 00:00:22.720
If they've lost everything, it
takes real identity proofing,

9
00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:27.600
in person or through Entra's new account
recovery, followed by a one-time pass.

10
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:33.400
What never counts: email, caller ID,
knowledge questions or a manager's say-so.

11
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:37.562
The ticket records which rung was
used, never the evidence itself.

12
00:00:37.562 --> 00:00:39.700
Then the ticket has to close properly.
